 export const sectionPages = [
   {
     slug: 'media-archives-and-memory',
-    title: 'Media, Archives and Memory',
+    metaTitle: '640 × 480, Pixilation and Picarty | Media Archives',
+    description: 'How the 640 × 480 image archive, Pixilation player and Picarty Mashup use sequence, software and participation to change photographs.',
+    h1: 'Digital image archives, pixilation and participatory photography',
+    introduction: [
+      'A digital photograph has more than one life. There is the moment it records. Then there are the decisions that determine its future: which files survive, how they are arranged, what an interface lets the viewer do, and whether other people can add to the image.',
+      { parts: [
+        'Three related projects make those decisions visible. ',
+        { emphasis: '640 × 480' },
+        ' is a long-running photographic archive and time-based work. Pixilation supplies a player for viewing its sequences. ',
+        { emphasis: 'Narcissus As Narcosis' },
+        ' is a separate participatory project whose Mashup application now has a web presence at Picarty. The projects share an interest in images as things people use, while their sources and forms remain distinct.',
+      ] },
+    ],
     sections: [
       {
-        title: 'The record is made twice',
+        title: '640 × 480: the sequence is the work',
+        paragraphs: [
+          { parts: [
+            'Goldberg began making ',
+            { emphasis: '640 × 480' },
+            ' images in Shanghai in 2002, using a small early digital camera. Its frames measured 640 by 480 pixels. At the time, an image that size could occupy much of a computer screen. The limits of the camera remain legible in the archive: noise, unstable colour and a small field of detail.',
+          ] },
+          'Looking at the files in quick succession revealed another possibility. Still frames began to behave like movement. Goldberg started photographing gestures, walks and changing rooms with the next frame in mind. An individual image could be held, but a sequence could carry time.',
+          'The full body of photographs, the chronological albums, any later selected sequence and a single frame are different ways of encountering the material. Selection makes a work legible; it also changes what the archive appears to remember. The record includes gaps. It cannot show an event the camera did not capture or an image that did not survive.',
+          { parts: [
+            'This is why ',
+            { emphasis: '640 × 480' },
+            ' needs more than a gallery of attractive stills. A frame can serve as evidence of a moment. Sequence gives that moment duration. The accumulated archive shows what repeated acts of looking leave behind.',
+          ] },
+        ],
+      },
+      {
+        title: 'Pixilation: the viewer sets the time',
         paragraphs: [
-          'A photograph records a moment. A system decides what that moment can become.',
-          'The camera sets the image size. The photographer chooses where to point it. An archive preserves some pictures and loses others. An interface controls the order, pace and context in which they return. None of these acts is neutral. Each one changes the record.',
-          '640 x 480 began with a cheap used camera. In 2002, after his Shanghai flat was robbed, Jared Goldberg bought a Pretec DC530 from a colleague. The camera produced images at 640 by 480 pixels, or 0.3 megapixels. That was also a common computer screen size at the time.',
-          'The limits were visible. The images were small. Their colour was unstable. Digital noise spread across each frame. Yet these flaws gave the pictures their character. They also fixed the work to a brief point in the history of consumer photography, when a digital camera still had to work hard to make a JPEG.',
-          'At first, Goldberg used the camera to take separate pictures. Then he pressed an arrow key while viewing them on his laptop. Because the files were so small, the images changed at once. One picture gave way to the next. Still photographs became movement.',
-          'That accident changed the practice.',
-          'Goldberg began to shoot with sequence in mind. A gesture could continue across several frames. A walk could become a pulse. A room could shift around a person. Daily life could appear as a rough form of animation known as pixilation.',
-          'The result is not a film in the usual sense. It is a long record built from separate moments over more than two decades. Its gaps matter as much as its continuity. A missing image may mark a lost file, a private event or a day when no picture was made. The archive does not offer complete memory. It shows how memory is built from what remains.',
+          { parts: [
+            'At ',
+            { link: { label: 'Pixilation.org, the 640 × 480 player', href: 'https://pixilation.org/' } },
+            ', the viewer can move through images instead of scanning thumbnails. Pace changes the reading. Rapid playback draws out rhythm and recurrence. Slower playback restores the weight of a particular face, street or gesture. Direction changes the apparent course of an action.',
+          ] },
+          'The viewer can make a temporary edit by moving through the material, but cannot make missing files reappear. The interface gives the archive a form without turning the historical record into a complete account of the past. Its controls are part of how the work is experienced.',
         ],
       },
       {
-        title: 'The player is part of the work',
+        title: 'Picarty: the image becomes a shared decision',
         paragraphs: [
-          'Pixilation.org does not present the archive as a wall of thumbnails. It gives the images time.',
-          'The player lets the viewer move through the sequence. Direction and speed affect what can be seen. Fast movement reveals rhythm. Slow movement returns attention to single frames. Moving backward can turn cause into effect and make a familiar action strange.',
-          'The player does more than show the files. It lets the viewer help edit the flow. It keeps the files, but it also stages their return.',
-          'This is the central question of Media, Archives and Memory: when does a collection of files become a record, and what happens when the tool used to preserve that record also changes how it is read?',
-          '640 x 480 gives one answer. Memory does not live in the image alone. It also lives in the sequence, the missing frames and the machine that brings the archive back to life.',
+          { parts: [
+            { emphasis: 'Narcissus As Narcosis' },
+            ' asks a different question: who acts on an image, and who is allowed to call the result a work? The earlier Mashup application and the current ',
+            { link: { label: 'Picarty/Mashup web interface', href: 'https://picarty.com/' } },
+            ' belong to this larger project, but they are distinct versions.',
+          ] },
+          "A participation session has several layers: a source capture, a participant's crop or mask, a generated composite, and any later selection by the artist. Those layers should remain visible when an image is described or exhibited. The person who supplies or changes an image, the software that combines it, and the artist who selects an outcome do different things. Their roles cannot be collapsed into one signature.",
+          { parts: [
+            'Picarty also changes the position of the viewer. With ',
+            { emphasis: '640 × 480' },
+            ', a visitor navigates an existing record. With Mashup, a participant helps produce an image that did not exist before the encounter. Both projects make the interface consequential. They do so through different kinds of agency.',
+          ] },
+          { parts: [
+            "Goldberg's ",
+            { link: { label: 'essay on Marshall McLuhan and the medium', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/writing/medium-is-the-message/' } },
+            ' sets out a wider theory of how a medium shapes perception. The projects here provide particular cases: the low-resolution camera, the sequential player and the participatory composite. Each places a different decision between the world and the picture.',
+          ] },
         ],
       },
     ],
     links: [
