   {
     slug: 'community-service',
-    title: 'Community Service',
+    metaTitle: 'The Money Club and Capability Works | Learning and Work',
+    description: 'An account of The Money Club’s youth financial-literacy pilot and Capability Works, a proposed approach to employment by design.',
+    h1: 'What does a person need in order to act?',
+    introduction: [
+      "An opportunity is shaped by its conditions. A young person can be told what a margin is, but learns more when a price has to meet a cost and another person's choice. A worker may be able to do a task, but be excluded by the hours, transport or noise attached to the job.",
+      'The Money Club and Capability Works approach different problems through the design of those conditions. Their statuses matter: one has run a first youth-program pilot; the other remains a proposed employment and housing model.',
+    ],
     sections: [
       {
-        title: 'Build it, then test it',
+        title: 'The Money Club: learning inside a small economy',
         paragraphs: [
-          'People learn best when their choices have real effects. People can work when jobs fit what they can do. Both need clear rules, useful tools and fast feedback.',
-          'Community Service brings two civic projects together. The Money Club tests these ideas with young people. Capability Works applies them to jobs and homes. One is a live program. The other is still a plan. Both start with the same view: when people are shut out or underused, the fault may lie in the system around them.',
+          { parts: [
+            { link: { label: 'The Money Club', href: 'https://the-money-club.org/' } },
+            ' teaches financial literacy through making, pricing, communication and testing. In August 2026, its first week-long program ran at the UTSU Student Commons in Toronto. Students researched needs, developed offers, examined costs and brought the offers to a Maker Market.',
+          ] },
+          'The market used virtual cash. This let students see peers make choices with a limited budget while keeping the financial stakes low. A purchase with virtual cash is stronger feedback than a compliment. It is still a classroom signal, and it does not establish what the same buyer would pay with their own money in another setting.',
+          'That distinction makes the pilot useful. The program can ask where students needed help, whether they understood the difference between revenue and profit, how they worked together, and what a later session should change. The first run tested a method of learning. A repeatable program will require documentation, new facilitators and further evidence.',
+          { parts: [
+            'Goldberg gives his own account in ',
+            { link: { label: 'The Money Club: Maiden Voyage', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/projects/the-money-club/maiden-voyage/' } },
+            '. His ',
+            { link: { label: 'essay on a deployable education system', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/writing/the-money-club-as-a-deployable-education-system/' } },
+            ' describes an ambition beyond one week in Toronto. This page records the difference between the completed pilot and that longer ambition.',
+          ] },
         ],
       },
       {
-        title: 'The Money Club',
+        title: 'Capability Works: redesigning the job',
         paragraphs: [
-          'Most financial education starts with rules. Save. Avoid debt. Make a budget. The rules may be sound, but students have no place to test them.',
-          'The Money Club starts with a live task.',
-          'Young people spot a need. They make a product, set a price and meet a buyer. Costs go out. Sales come in. Each choice leaves a mark. The next choice can improve because the feedback is real.',
-          'Design, money and AI become tools for the work. Students do not pretend to make a product. They make it. They do not guess if an idea has worth. They place it in front of someone and find out.',
-          'This changes the lesson. Money is no longer just a set of good habits. It becomes a record of choice, risk and shared work.',
-          'The Club is a nonprofit. Its aim is not to use student work for gain. Its aim is to make the full loop clear while the stakes are low enough for safe mistakes.',
+          { parts: [
+            { link: { label: 'Capability Works', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/projects/capital-works/' } },
+            " is a proposal for matching people's capabilities and preferred working conditions to tasks with real demand. It begins with a basic observation: a job description bundles the useful task together with a schedule, setting, pace and social demands. Some of those conditions are essential. Others may be inherited habits of the organization.",
+          ] },
+          'The proposed model would examine tasks and conditions separately, then build work with the person who will do it. It also imagines a relationship between paid work and more stable housing. That relationship has not been demonstrated by an operating employment platform. It will require partners, worker participation, research and a pilot that follows what happens after a placement.',
+          { parts: [
+            "Goldberg's ",
+            { link: { label: "essay on the design of work", href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/writing/the-future-of-work-is-a-design-problem/' } },
+            " explains the problem from the worker's point of view. A later ",
+            { link: { label: 'essay on dignity and continuity', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/writing/dignity-is-a-systems-output/' } },
+            ' asks what happens when a job can be performed but transport, scheduling and support fail to meet. Here the institutional question is concrete: who has the power and information to repair the connection?',
+          ] },
         ],
       },
       {
-        title: 'Capability Works',
+        title: 'What joins these projects',
         paragraphs: [
-          'Capability Works begins with employment. It asks why a person who wants to work can still be left out.',
-          'The usual answer blames the person. They may lack a degree. They may have a health need or a limit. Capability Works looks at the job instead.',
-          'Many jobs bundle tasks in a poor way. They ask one worker to meet a fixed mix of speed, motion, memory, speech and time. A person may do key parts of the work well but still fail the whole test.',
-          'The plan maps what each person can do. It also maps real market demand. Tasks can then be split and rebuilt. Tools, training and the work site can fit the match. Income would fund the work first. In time, it could also help fund stable homes.',
-          'This is not yet a proven employment platform. It still needs research, partners, pilots and proof. That point must stay clear. Civic work should state what is real and what is still an idea.',
-          'Together, the two projects treat service as system design. The Money Club lets young people learn inside a real money loop. Capability Works asks how a fairer loop could link work, pay and homes. Both move past advice and good will. They build the terms that let people act.',
+          'One project gives young people a controlled way to test an offer. The other asks how adults might be given better terms for paid contribution. They differ in maturity, audience and risk. Both suggest that advice is limited when the environment gives someone no usable way to act.',
+          'For The Money Club, the next evidence is another iteration with clear observations of learning and participation. For Capability Works, it is a participatory design process and a small test of sustained, fairly paid work. In both cases, the system should learn from the people whose lives it organizes.',
         ],
       },
     ],
     links: [
-      { label: 'Read about The Money Club', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/projects/the-money-club/' },
-      { label: 'Read about Capability Works', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/projects/capital-works/' },
-      { label: 'View all public projects', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/projects/' },
