   {
     slug: 'systems-and-institutions',
-    title: 'Systems and Institutions',
+    metaTitle: 'How Institutions Make Decisions | Systems and Retail',
+    description: 'A comparative reading of design and manufacturing in China, private label at Loblaw and retail media at Walmart and Canadian Tire.',
+    h1: 'How institutions make decisions',
+    introduction: [
+      'A product on a shelf can look like one decision. It is usually the end of many: what a factory can make, what a buyer can stock, what a store can sell, what a customer notices and what the next report measures. The same product changes meaning as it crosses those thresholds.',
+      { parts: [
+        'Goldberg has worked on different sides of this chain: teaching and product development in Shanghai; private-label portfolios at Loblaw; retail media at Walmart; and in-store media systems at Canadian Tire. His ',
+        { link: { label: 'first-person work accounts', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/index.html' } },
+        ' describe what he saw inside those organizations. This page compares the decision structures those accounts bring into view.',
+      ] },
+    ],
     sections: [
       {
-        title: 'Rules beat intent',
+        title: 'The factory: what can be repeated?',
         paragraphs: [
-          'Large systems do not run on good intentions. They run on rules, incentives, roles, habits and flows of information.',
-          'Jared Goldberg saw this from both ends of a supply chain. In China, he worked in design and manufacturing. In Canada, he worked inside large retailers. One side made the product. The other chose what earned shelf space, funding and attention.',
-          'The jobs changed. The lesson held. Results depend on how the whole chain fits.',
+          'In China, Goldberg taught design and worked at Bestway, a maker of toys and sporting goods. His account of an innovation lab joins research, product design, manufacturing and the materials used to sell a product. A design can succeed on paper and still fail at tooling, cost, production or presentation.',
+          { parts: [
+            'This makes a factory an interesting site of authorship. A designer proposes a form. Production gives it tolerances and a price. A retailer asks for differentiation. The product that reaches a buyer is the result of negotiation among these demands. Goldberg describes that movement in his ',
+            { link: { label: 'account of China and supply chains', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/china.html' } },
+            '.',
+          ] },
         ],
       },
       {
-        title: 'China and manufacturing',
+        title: 'The shelf: who controls comparison?',
         paragraphs: [
-          'Goldberg moved to Shanghai in 2002. He first taught design. He then joined Bestway, a large maker of toys and sporting goods.',
-          'In manufacturing, design, tooling, production, logistics, marketing and retail must act as one. A poor choice at the start gets more costly at each step. A short feedback loop lets a team fix the work before the fault grows.',
-          'Scale sets up a clash. Plants need goods they can make again and again. Stores want new offers. Buyers want choice. The fix was not more looks. It was a clear plan for brands, goods and sales channels.',
-          'This work also changed how Goldberg saw innovation. An idea has little worth if a firm cannot use it. A good lab needs a clear goal, access to decision makers and a path from insight to production.',
+          'At Loblaw, the retailer did more than buy products. It could develop private-label offers, compare them with national brands and decide how the assortment would appear on its own shelves. A brand invests in being chosen; a retailer controls the setting in which the choice is made.',
+          { parts: [
+            'Internal decisions also have an audience. A proposal needs evidence, a financial case and people willing to act on it. In Goldberg\'s ',
+            { link: { label: 'Loblaw account', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/loblaw.html' } },
+            ', the competition for investment is as important as the competition between products. Shelf space is physical; attention and approval inside the company are scarce resources too.',
+          ] },
         ],
       },
       {
-        title: 'Canadian mass retail',
+        title: 'The store: who sets the default?',
         paragraphs: [
-          'At Loblaw, the work dealt with portfolios, private labels and bids for capital. Data was key. Yet facts did not move the firm on their own. Teams had to earn trust and make a case that peers could use. Loblaw showed how evidence, belief and decision rights work as one.',
-          'At Walmart, culture worked like an operating system. Cost rules shaped store layouts, supplier talks and daily rites. A familiar store reduced stress for the shopper. A clear promise could spread through a vast network because the rules did not drift.',
-          'At Canadian Tire, the dealer model gives local operators real power. The corporation supplies national scale. Its banners also serve distinct needs. Canadian Tire, SportChek, Mark’s and Gas+ do not create the same store trip. One media system must share a core and still fit each banner.',
-          'These cases show why an institution is more than a brand. It is a set of permissions. It decides who can act, what can change and which results count.',
+          { parts: [
+            "Goldberg's ",
+            { link: { label: 'Walmart account', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/walmart.html' } },
+            ' pays attention to repeated store patterns and operating rituals. Layout, price promises and routines can align many people without a new instruction for every choice. Scale depends partly on the ability to repeat a rule; it also makes exceptions harder to see.',
+          ] },
+          { parts: [
+            'At Canadian Tire, the dealer model introduces another form of control. Local operators have room to respond to their markets, while national banners and shared systems create a common network. An automotive errand, a sporting-goods purchase and a workwear trip each put a shopper in a different frame of mind. A media placement that works in one setting cannot simply be assumed to work in another. Goldberg discusses those conditions in his ',
+            { link: { label: 'Canadian Tire account', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/canadian-tire.html' } },
+            '.',
+          ] },
         ],
       },
       {
-        title: 'The same question at every scale',
+        title: 'The measure: what result counts?',
         paragraphs: [
-          'The study of systems is not apart from the work. It is how the work gets done.',
-          'Where does information enter? Who gets to choose? What does each side gain or risk? Which result comes back as feedback? Where does one choice add cost to the next step?',
-          'Those questions apply to a factory, a store network, a civic program and an artist-made institution. The goal may change. The need for clear rules does not.',
+          'Retail media joins physical space to advertising and measurement. A screen can be sold as an impression; a merchant still needs the right stock, price and store experience. A measured sale may include products that were never advertised. Counting what happened is different from showing what the media caused.',
+          'The question for an institution is therefore practical: whose objective is being optimized, who can change the conditions and what evidence would show an improvement for the shopper and the business? A dashboard can report an outcome while leaving the decision that produced it obscure.',
+          { parts: [
+            'Goldberg develops the incentive argument in ',
+            { link: { label: 'What Real Systems Taught Me About Incentives', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/writing/real-systems-incentives/' } },
+            ". The cases gathered here add a comparative view. The factory, the shelf and the store have different owners and constraints. Each decides what becomes visible and what can be done next. These are accounts of Goldberg's work, not statements on behalf of the organizations named.",
+          ] },
         ],
       },
     ],
     links: [
-      { label: 'Read China: Supply Chain as Infrastructure', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/china.html' },
-      { label: 'Read Loblaw: Learning How Big Systems Actually Work', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/loblaw.html' },
-      { label: 'Read Walmart: Culture as a Cost Structure', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/walmart.html' },
-      { label: 'Read Canadian Tire: Inside a Uniquely Canadian System', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/canadian-tire.html' },
+      { label: 'Read the China account', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/china.html' },
+      { label: 'Read the Loblaw account', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/loblaw.html' },
+      { label: 'Read the Walmart account', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/walmart.html' },
+      { label: 'Read the Canadian Tire account', href: 'https://jaredgoldberg.ca/work/canadian-tire.html' },
     ],
