   {
     slug: 'art',
-    title: 'Art',
+    metaTitle: 'Duchamped, The Pitch and Artistic Value | Art',
+    description: 'An introduction to Duchamped, the historical stage name Jared the Jew, and artworks that test authorship and value.',
+    h1: 'Art and the manufacture of value',
+    introduction: [
+      "Jared Goldberg's art uses more than objects. It uses names, reproductions, storefronts, prices, interfaces and the expectation that an artist will explain what any of these are worth. Some works invite play. Others make the transaction itself uncomfortable. The frame around an artwork can carry as much of the argument as the object inside it.",
+      { parts: [
+        { link: { label: 'Duchamped', href: 'https://duchamped.com/' } },
+        " is the principal home for the art practice. This page is a route into its questions and related projects. Artwork records, available editions and the work's own promotional language belong at their source.",
+      ] },
+    ],
     sections: [
       {
-        title: 'The name is the asset',
+        title: 'To Duchamp: changing the frame',
         paragraphs: [
-          'Jared Goldberg’s art asks a blunt question. Who can name a work, set its price and make that price count?',
-          'The answer does not sit in one object. It sits in a system made from the person, the artist, the institution and the market.',
-          'Jared Goldberg is the legal person. Jared the Jew is the artist and public name. Duchamped is the institution that shows and certifies the work. The Pitch turns the work into a financial claim. Each part lends force to the rest.',
+          'Goldberg uses “to Duchamp” as a verb for taking a familiar thing and making it operate inside another frame. The thing may be an image, a product, a name or a market convention. Its source can remain recognizable while its use and implied value change.',
+          { parts: [
+            "The verb is Goldberg's artistic usage, explained in the ",
+            { link: { label: 'Duchamped manifesto', href: 'https://duchamped.com/duchamped/' } },
+            '. It is useful because it names an operation rather than a style. A reproduced image, an inflated object and a staged investment page do not have to look alike to ask who is allowed to assign meaning and price.',
+          ] },
+          'That question has limits. Appropriation has a source, an owner and consequences. The relation between an artwork and the thing it borrows must be examined case by case; the verb does not settle it.',
         ],
       },
       {
-        title: 'To Duchamp',
+        title: "The artist's names",
         paragraphs: [
-          'Goldberg describes his method with a verb: to Duchamp.',
-          'To Duchamp is to take a thing and build a new frame around it. The source can stay in view. Its owner, use and worth can still change.',
-          'The act can use an image, an object, a brand, a myth or a price. Most of all, it can use Goldberg’s own name.',
-          '“Jared the Jew” began as words used to hurt him. He took the slur and made it his mark. The insult became his public name and artistic material.',
-          'That act does not make the name safe. Goldberg can claim it, print it and build an institution around it. He cannot control each reading. A bigot can say it. A Jewish viewer can reject it. A museum can fear it. A collector can buy it.',
-          'Jared the Jew is a self-portrait that Goldberg can make but never own in full. That lack of control is part of the work.',
+          { parts: [
+            'Goldberg has worked under more than one public identity. ',
+            { emphasis: 'The Subversive Artist' },
+            ' appears as a persona in the current art presentation. ',
+            { emphasis: 'Jared the Jew' },
